    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Best... I is a 1992 compilation by The Smiths, released by WEA after the band’s back catalogue changed hands. It collects 14 of the group’s best-known tracks from their 1983–1987 run, and it became a major commercial success, reaching No. 1 on the UK Albums Chart and No. 139 in the United States.\n\nMusically, the set is a concise overview of The Smiths’ signature sound: jangling guitars, melodic bass lines, and Morrissey’s sharply literate, emotionally fraught lyrics. The tracklist includes defining songs such as “This Charming Man,” “How Soon Is Now?,” “Stop Me If You Think You’ve Heard This One Before,” and “Please, Please, Please, Let Me Get What I Want,” making it one of the most accessible entry points into the band’s catalog.\n\nBecause it is a hits collection rather than a studio album, Best... I works less as a unified artistic statement than as a summary of The Smiths’ impact on British indie rock. Its chart performance and continued reissues reflect the enduring demand for the band’s most celebrated singles and album tracks.",
